WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:07.650
Film Courage: This may be an old wives’
tale about unhappy writers having more depth

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:12.320
in which to write about, more in which to
pull from? Whereas the happy writer is just

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:18.029
scratching the surface and it might be too
much of a movie of the week instead of something

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:25.140
that pulls at your emotional core and you
can put yourself in the character’s shoes?

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.880
I don’t know, can we dispel this (that you
have to be unhappy)?

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.129
Dr. Ken Atchity, Producer/Author:&nbsp; This is
a famous dilemma that people have been talking

00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:40.109
about my whole lifetime. A book came out a
few years ago called&nbsp;The Drama of the Gifted

00:00:40.109 --> 00:00:49.930
Child&nbsp;by&nbsp;Alice Miller. It’s a very interesting
book and it basically says that writers should

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:58.359
fear therapy because it might therapize, it
might take away their angst from which came

00:00:58.359 --> 00:01:07.410
all their brilliant ideas. And it’s simply
not true because there are just too many examples

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:12.910
of productive writers who have plenty of angst
and one of my favorite examples is&nbsp;Stephen

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:16.450
King&nbsp;who published in&nbsp;my magazine Dreamworks.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.490
We sent out a letter to artists all over the
world including him.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:28.920
Could you please tell us whether dreams have
any influence on your creativity and if so,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:35.960
give us an example of a dream and a creative
work that came from it?

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:41.510
So&nbsp;Fellini&nbsp;sent us a cartoon that he dreamed
in the middle of the night that led to&nbsp;8

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:48.760
1/2&nbsp;(his movie) and we got great stuff from
all kinds of people. And Stephen King six

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:53.360
months later after everyone else sent us a
very short letter and said:

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:00.250
This is my constant nightmare. I am sitting
alone in the attic typing away and a little

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:06.960
door in the floor of the attic opens and a
hideous face comes out of the floor and I

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.860
start typing as fast as I can because the
faster I type the more the door closes and

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.120
if I slow down the face keeps coming out.
And then he says:

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.270
Does that count?

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:27.990
And it’s an example of what you’re talking
about because he has plenty of angst going

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:35.160
on. He has plenty of terror and fear and dark
things in some of his most brilliant work

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:44.280
like the&nbsp;SHAWSHANK REDEMPTION&nbsp;and&nbsp;THE SHINING.
And he’s not an unhappy writer. He knows

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:50.250
that he needs to put the time in every day
and he’s figured it out and he’s prolific

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:55.560
and so on.
So there are just too many examples of balanced

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.940
writers, let’s call them mentally balanced
writers. One of my favorite statements from

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:06.930
the world of art is&nbsp;Salvador Dali&nbsp;who said&nbsp;The
difference between myself and a madman is

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:14.160
that I am not mad.&nbsp;And I love that because
only an artist who knows how close sanity

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:21.490
he is to insanity knows what that means. Like
he’s one of those mad men who is not mad.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:27.270
Whereas a lot of other mad men are mad. And
okay, they kill themselves or they kill somebody

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.530
else.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:37.130
And so it’s all about knowing yourself,
it’s all about how your mind works and proving

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:45.260
it and testing it until you look back and
you go&nbsp;My gosh I’ve written all these books.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.130
I’ve got all these things going on. And
I don’t think I’m crazy and on the other

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:57.239
hand I don’t think I’m sane either.&nbsp;It’s
like you’ve just figured it out and it doesn’t

00:03:57.239 --> 00:04:02.489
mean that you’re not having dark spells.
It’s just that you kind of look at your

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:06.760
dark spells from the outside instead of from
the inside.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:13.330
It’s very common in meditation and yoga
to understand that you can either be inside

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:19.079
yourself all the time and drive yourself crazy
by letting your mind run it (what’s going

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:25.800
on). Or you can stay above your mind by all
these thoughts going by and all this stuff

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:33.389
and recognize that you (the one looking) is
in charge, not all of the thoughts.

00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:39.419
And&nbsp;A Writer’s Time&nbsp;has all these theories
about how the creative mind works which is

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:45.370
what I call the&nbsp;managing editor,&nbsp;looking
at the fight going on inside your mind and

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:51.199
realizing that this can be controlled if you
trick the two sides of the mind and force

00:04:51.199 --> 00:04:57.710
them to work together. That’s kind of what
it’s all about to become happy and productive

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:02.849
at the same time and it seems to work for
a lot of people.

